! lve_chain_usa.strap -- LOAD VALUE EFFECTIVE through a chain of LVEs,
! and the one-millisecond end of a chain that never ends.
!
! WHY IT EXISTS, AND WHO ASKED FOR IT.  MCP did.  Printed p.159 line 8
! is "LVE,0,K IOD RN-.32", and the half-word it names is printed p.177
! line 15, assembled "LVE,0,$" -- an LVE.  So MCP's LVE is a CHAIN by
! construction.  Its address is not really "$": p.177 lines 9 and 11
! ("SVA,2,K IOD RN" / "SVA,2,K IOD RN-.32") write an IOD parameter's
! address into lines 16 and 15 before they run, so the chain goes on
! into the user's IOD, and p.177 lines 17-18 ("BUSAZ,$+.32",
! "BADZ,$+.32", half-length branches to the next instruction) clear USA
! and AD whatever the chain did.  Until 2026-11-06 the model refused
! every chained LVE with OP (decks/lve_indirect.strap said so), and
! MCP's body stopped there: "OP INTERRUPT" at 220023.40.
!
! WHAT THE MANUAL SAYS, Reference Manual printed p.29, read in ink:
!   - LVE, "which extends the indirect addressing to another level, is
!     always executed";
!   - "The index register in which the final effective address is to
!     be stored is specified by the index field J of the original LOAD
!     VALUE EFFECTIVE instruction.  The fields J of all subsequent
!     instructions encountered in the operation are ignored.";
!   - a loop is terminated "if still active, after one millisecond and
!     indicator USA, unended sequence of addresses, will be turned on.
!     At time of termination, the final effective address is placed in
!     the index register specified by the J field of the original
!     instruction."
!
! WHAT EACH BLOCK ESTABLISHES
!   A  Two levels that end: START's LVE reads C1 (an LVE), which names
!      C2 (an LVE), which names T (B,DATUM).  $X1 := DATUM.  The J
!      fields of C1 and C2 name $X6 and $X7, which must stay zero.
!   B  An intermediate LVE's OWN index is honoured: D1 is
!      LVE,$X7,D2-1.0($X3) with $X3 = 1.0, so the chain reaches D2
!      (B,PASS) only if D1's I field is applied.  $X2 := PASS.  Without
!      the index it reaches T (B,DATUM) instead, a different answer.
!   C  USA is OFF after chains that end (BUSAZ to BAD).
!   D  A one-word loop: LOOP is "LVE,$X5,$".  The original's J is $X4,
!      so $X4 := LOOP's address and USA comes on; $X5 must stay zero.
!      With a one-word loop the "final effective address" is LOOP
!      whichever way the phrase is read -- the location last read, or
!      the address last formed -- so this does not decide between them.
!
! INJECTIONS: measured, and tests/run_tests.sh records them beside its
! entry.

        SLC, (8)2400.0

! ---- A. two levels that end ----------------------------------------
START   LVE, $X1, C1
        SV,  $X1, R1

! ---- B. an intermediate LVE's own index ----------------------------
        LX,  $X3, XOFF
        LVE, $X2, D1
        SV,  $X2, R2

! ---- C. no USA from a chain that ends ------------------------------
        BUSAZ, BAD

! ---- D. a one-word loop ---------------------------------------------
        LVE, $X4, LOOP
        SV,  $X4, R3
        SV,  $X5, R4                 ! LOOP's own J: must be untouched
        SV,  $X6, R5                 ! C1's J
        SV,  $X7, R6                 ! C2's and D1's J
        BUSAZ, PASS                  ! USA must be ON
BAD     B,   $

! ---- the chains -------------------------------------------------------
C1      LVE, $X6, C2
C2      LVE, $X7, T
T       B,   DATUM
D1      LVE, $X7, D2-1.0($X3)
D2      B,   PASS
LOOP    LVE, $X5, $

XOFF    XW,  1.0, 0, 0, 0            ! D1's index value
DATUM   DD(BU,64,8), 0
R1      VF,  0
R2      VF,  0
R3      VF,  0
R4      VF,  0
R5      VF,  0
R6      VF,  0
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
